 questions: [
  ["A 60-year-old with a recent bleeding peptic ulcer asks for an NSAID after an extraction. Which of the following best explains why it is unsuitable?","COX-1 inhibition reduces prostaglandins that protect the gastric mucosa",["COX-2 inhibition increases prostaglandins that erode the gastric mucosa","Lipoxygenase inhibition increases leukotrienes that erode the gastric mucosa","Acetylation of thromboxane directly digests the gastric mucosa"],"Constitutive COX-1 makes the prostaglandins that maintain mucus, bicarbonate and mucosal blood flow. COX-2 is mainly induced by inflammation, and selective blockers spare the stomach to a degree."],
  ["A healthy 24-year-old has 4.8 surgically removed and moderate-to-severe pain is expected. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line prescription?","Ibuprofen 400 mg plus acetaminophen 1000 mg every 6 hours",["Acetaminophen 300 mg with codeine 30 mg every 4 hours","Oxycodone 5 mg with acetaminophen every 6 hours","Tramadol alone every 6 hours"],"This combination has the strongest evidence in third molar pain, with an NNT of 1.5, and fewer adverse effects than opioid combinations. Codeine combinations are common by habit but are not first-line."],
  ["According to Health Canada, the recommended maximum daily dose of acetaminophen for a healthy adult is:","4 g",["1 g","2.4 g","8 g"],"Health Canada gives 4 g (4000 mg) per day for healthy adults. The limit may be lower with liver disease or heavy alcohol use, and 2400 mg is a figure for prescription ibuprofen."],
  ["In Canada, the maximum daily dose of non-prescription ibuprofen for an adult is:","1200 mg",["400 mg","2400 mg","3200 mg"],"Non-prescription labelling allows up to 1200 mg per day for short-term use. The prescription maximum is 2400 mg per day, which Health Canada advises against in patients with cardiovascular disease or risk factors."],
  ["A 67-year-old on warfarin with an INR of 2.6 has toothache pending extraction of 4.6. The most appropriate analgesic is:","Acetaminophen",["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Acetylsalicylic acid"],"Acetaminophen has little platelet or gastric effect, though sustained high doses can raise the INR modestly. The three NSAID options add to bleeding risk by impairing platelets and the gastric mucosa."],
  ["A 42-year-old with asthma and nasal polyps had severe wheeze after acetylsalicylic acid. The most appropriate analgesic after extraction of 1.6 is:","Acetaminophen",["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Ketorolac"],"This history suggests aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, in which COX inhibitors can trigger bronchospasm. All the other options are NSAIDs, so the reaction can cross over."],
  ["A 72-year-old takes ramipril and hydrochlorothiazide. A dentist considers several days of ibuprofen. The specific risk created by this combination is:","Acute kidney injury from reduced glomerular filtration pressure",["Acute liver failure from depletion of hepatic glutathione","Hyperglycaemia from reduced pancreatic insulin release","Bronchospasm from excess leukotriene production"],"NSAIDs constrict the afferent arteriole, ACE inhibitors dilate the efferent arteriole, and diuretics reduce volume, so filtration pressure falls. Glutathione depletion belongs to acetaminophen overdose."],
  ["A 36-year-old taking paroxetine reports no benefit from acetaminophen with codeine after an extraction, though she took it correctly. The best explanation is that paroxetine:","Inhibits CYP2D6, reducing conversion of codeine to morphine",["Induces CYP2D6, increasing conversion of codeine to morphine","Blocks mu receptors, preventing morphine from binding to them","Inhibits CYP3A4, preventing the absorption of codeine"],"Paroxetine is a strong CYP2D6 inhibitor, so the patient behaves like a poor metaboliser and little morphine forms. Induction would increase, not reduce, the effect."],
  ["A 28-year-old becomes very drowsy with pinpoint pupils and slow breathing after usual doses of acetaminophen with codeine. Genotyping later shows extra functional CYP2D6 gene copies. The most likely mechanism is:","Excess conversion of codeine to morphine",["Accumulation of the toxic metabolite NAPQI","Reduced conversion of codeine to morphine","Failure of the kidneys to excrete acetaminophen"],"Ultrarapid metabolisers convert more codeine to morphine, which can produce opioid toxicity at normal doses. NAPQI causes liver injury, not pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression."],
  ["A 9-year-old needs an analgesic after extraction of 8.5, and the parent requests codeine. In line with Health Canada advice, the most appropriate response is to:","Recommend weight-based acetaminophen or ibuprofen rather than codeine",["Prescribe codeine at half the adult dose for two days","Prescribe codeine only if it is combined with acetaminophen","Recommend codeine syrup in place of a codeine tablet"],"Health Canada advises against codeine under age 12 because ultrarapid metabolism can cause serious breathing problems. Lowering the dose or changing the formulation does not remove that genetic risk."],
  ["A 30-year-old takes a large overdose of acetaminophen. Which of the following best explains the resulting liver injury?","Depleted glutathione allows NAPQI to bind to hepatocyte proteins",["Reduced prostaglandins lower blood flow through the hepatic artery","Accumulated acetaminophen directly blocks hepatocyte sodium pumps","Increased glucuronidation consumes the available hepatic energy stores"],"NAPQI is normally neutralised by glutathione. When stores run out, it binds cell proteins and causes centrilobular necrosis, and N-acetylcysteine works by replenishing glutathione."],
  ["Under RCDSO guidance, the general maximum for a single prescription of a combination containing codeine 30 mg is:","24 tablets",["12 tablets","36 tablets","60 tablets"],"RCDSO suggests 24 tablets for codeine 30 mg combinations and for oxycodone 5 mg combinations, and 36 for codeine 15 mg combinations. Exceeding them should be the exception, justified by professional judgment."],
  ["In third molar surgery trials, ibuprofen 400 mg plus acetaminophen 1000 mg had an NNT of 1.5 for at least 50% pain relief, compared with placebo. Which of the following best interprets this result?","Treating about 3 patients gives 2 more responders than placebo would",["Treating about 15 patients gives 1 more responder than placebo would","About 1.5% of treated patients achieve relief beyond that of placebo","About 1 in 1.5 treated patients has an adverse effect from treatment"],"NNT is the reciprocal of the absolute benefit: 1/1.5 is about 67%, matching 73% minus 7%. It describes benefit, so it says nothing directly about the harm rate."],
  ["A 45-year-old takes two tablets of acetaminophen 300 mg with codeine 30 mg every 4 hours around the clock and adds acetaminophen 1000 mg twice daily. His daily acetaminophen intake is:","5.6 g, which exceeds the 4 g maximum",["3.6 g, which is within the 4 g maximum","2.0 g, which is within the 4 g maximum","4.0 g, which equals the 4 g maximum"],"Twelve tablets at 300 mg give 3.6 g, and the added doses give 2 g more. Only counting the combination tablets gives the falsely reassuring 3.6 g, which is why every source must be added up."],
  ["A 29-year-old with symptomatic irreversible pulpitis of 4.6 requests something stronger than ibuprofen. The most appropriate next step is to:","Provide pulpectomy or extraction and optimise non-opioid analgesia",["Prescribe oxycodone until an appointment becomes available","Prescribe codeine in case non-opioid analgesia is not sufficient","Prescribe an antibiotic with codeine to shrink the infection"],"RCDSO guidance starts with removing the source of the pain, then uses non-opioids, with opioids third-line. Antibiotics do not relieve irreversible pulpitis, and the ADA 2024 guideline advises against just-in-case opioid prescriptions."]
 ]
